DevOps Internship - Week 3 Report

Topic : Shell Scripting, Automation & Web Servers

-> System Information Script
A Bash script was used to display basic information about the Ubuntu system. The script collects
information such as the current user, hostname, operating system/kernel, date and time, IP information, disk
usage, and memory usage.
Example commands used
chmod +x system_info.sh
./system_info.sh

-> Automated Backup
A Bash backup script was prepared to automate the backup process. The activity creates a backup
directory, copies the project files, and compresses the backup into a .tar.gz archive. The generated archive
provides a single file that can be stored or transferred.
Backup flow
Project files → Backup directory → Compressed .tar.gz archive

-> User and Directory Automation
A script to create users and directories. This reduces repetitive manual
work when setting up a project environment. The directory structure used for the internship includes project,
script, log, and backup locations.

-> log cleanup
A separate Bash script is used for the required log-management exercise. Its purpose is to identify old log
files and remove them according to the selected retention condition. The script should be tested carefully so
that only intended files are removed.
These two activities demonstrate how Bash can be used to automate common Linux administration tasks.

-> Cron Job Automation
Cron was used to schedule the backup script. Instead of running the backup manually, a Cron entry can
execute the script at a defined time or interval. The Cron configuration was checked using crontab -l and the
Cron service status was verified.
Verification commands
crontab -l
sudo systemctl status cron --no-pager

-> Apache Web Server
Apache was selected for the web server activity. The server was installed on Ubuntu and used to host a
simple HTML page. After deployment, the Apache service status and port 80 were checked.
Typical verification commands
sudo systemctl status apache2 --no-pager
ss -tuln | grep ':80'

-> Concepts Covered
---Shell Scripting---
Shell scripting is a way to place Linux commands into a script so that a sequence of operations can be
repeated consistently. In this week's work, Bash scripts were used for system information, backups, user
and directory creation, and log cleanup.
---Variables---
Variables store values that a script can use later. They are useful for paths, filenames, dates, user input,
and configuration values.
---Loops---
Loops repeat a set of commands. They are useful when the same operation needs to be performed for
multiple files, directories, or values.
---Functions---
Functions group related commands into reusable sections. This can make a Bash script easier to read,
maintain, and test.
---Cron Jobs---
Cron provides scheduled execution of commands or scripts. It is useful for recurring tasks such as backups.

-> Apache, Nginx and Log Management
---Apache---
Apache is a web server used to serve web content over HTTP/HTTPS. It was used for the practical website
deployment in this week's activity.
---Nginx---
Nginx is another web server that can serve static content and also act as a reverse proxy. The official
assignment asks for understanding of both Apache and Nginx, while allowing either Apache or Nginx for the
practical installation.
---Log Management---
Log management involves monitoring, reviewing, retaining, and cleaning log files. The Week 3 log-cleanup
script demonstrates how an administrator can automate part of this process.

-> Conclusion
This week provided practical experience with Bash scripting, Linux automation, Cron scheduling, and web
server deployment. The activities showed how repetitive Linux tasks can be converted into scripts and
scheduled instead of being performed manually each time.